sum='On 27 October 1999 Prashant, a teenager, had gone to visit his friend in Ersama, a small town in Orissa (about 18 km from his village Kalikuda). That night a super cyclone with winds of 350 km per hour and torrential rain struck. Water entered his friend’s house; they climbed onto the roof. For two days they huddled there, cold and wet; uprooted coconut trees on the roof gave them tender coconuts to eat. When the water receded, Prashant waded back to his village through dead bodies and carcasses. His family was alive and sheltering in the Red Cross cyclone shelter, where about 2500 people were starving (86 villagers died, all 96 houses were washed away). Prashant organised a group of youths to force the merchant to give rice; they cooked food on fallen tree branches, cleaned the shelter, nursed the injured and took care of the dead. When military helicopters passed without dropping food, they made children lie in the sand with empty utensils on their stomachs – the helicopters then dropped food. Prashant set up a polythene shelter for orphans, persuaded the women to join an NGO’s food-for-work programme, organised sports and cricket for the children, and helped resettle orphans in their own community with childless widows instead of in institutions. Busy helping others, he healed his own wounded spirit.',
